    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_d1b854152c4dbedf298d8ece482a2dc5a4e06e145bedc82408ede71e67d9d3a1",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The Mellin–Plancherel isometry turns fixed-damping H-closure into spectral L2 convergence and factors each approximant into a zeta factor, a partial Möbius Dirichlet sum, and a linear denominator. Under RH, reciprocal-zeta convergence together with RH-implied Lindelöf domination constructs the fixed-epsilon limit; after the sum has disappeared into a zeta quotient, the functional-equation and gamma-function bound controls epsilon tending to zero without another use of RH.",
